[08:17.38]Passage One
[08:19.92]Welcome, everybody, to the guide for the Peak District. 
[08:23.77]Situated in the heart of England and covering parts of six counties, the Peak District is a place of outstanding natural beauty. 
[08:33.39]It is just five miles from the Sheffield City Center.
[08:37.13]A short break, or longer holiday, will allow you to explore some of England's most spectacular scenery and in the towns, villages and hamlets¡ªamong the prettiest in the country¡ªyou will find a warm and genuine welcome wherever you go. 
[08:54.19]Every taste is catered for and many exciting activities are available. 
[08:59.83]Here are some highlights of the most popular places to visit. 
[09:03.68]For the most active people, the following areas offer you excellent opportunities for walking, strolling, rock-climbing, caving, cycling, and hang-gliding.
[09:16.39]The first place I'd like to introduce to you is Bakewell. 
[09:20.25]This beautiful little town is renowned for a certain local food¡ªthe famous Bakewell pudding. 
[09:27.57]It's very delicious and steeped in history. 
[09:31.21]Bakewell's oldest building dates back to Henry ¢÷'s reign. 
[09:35.83]Just south of the town is the medieval Haddon Hall, a magnificent Tudor manor house complete with gardens, the most romantic in England. 
[09:45.26]On Sheffield's doorstep lies the Palace of the Peak. 
[09:49.36]Chatsworth is quite possibly the UK's most stunning country house and a ¡°must see¡± for any visitor. 
[09:57.04]The magnificent ground alone¡ªwith their formal gardens, fountains and maze¡ªis worth a visit.
[10:04.11]At the heart of the Peak District lies the Peak District National Park, the first of Britain's National Parks, established in 1951, to safeguard and preserve this beautiful region.
[10:18.76]Questions 9 to 11 are based on the passage you have just heard.
[10:23.25]9. What do we learn about the Peak District?
